  "prompt_text": "The figure places lightwave electronics in context between conventional electronics, ultrafast optics, and much higher-field relativistic optics or laser fusion. The left side is a map of operating regimes versus field strength and speed, spanning from MHz/GHz electronic devices to THz–PHz and beyond. In the center, a shaded region highlights lightwave electronics, where strong optical fields directly drive electron motion in atoms and solids on extremely short timescales. The sketches suggest electron dynamics over atomic distances in atoms and over a few angstroms in solids, with energies on the order of electron-volts per inverse angstrom, emphasizing that this regime connects microscopic motion to ultrafast control.\n\nThe right side visualizes the underlying solid-state process in a band structure picture. Two valence bands and a conduction band are shown as energy surfaces that evolve with time and wave vector. An optical field appears to move carriers through the bands, generating transitions near a point marked V and producing trajectories in the conduction band. The label “Bragg reflection” indicates that electrons driven to the edge of the band are reflected back in momentum space. Overall, the figure conveys how intense light can control electronic motion in crystals on femtosecond or even attosecond timescales.",
